2. All 96 threads execute the same unconditional barrier, with no early returns. The barrier makes those preceding shared-memory writes visible to thread zero before its reads. Slots at indices `valid` through 95 may remain uninitialized, but they are never read.
3. Only thread zero writes `*output`. When `valid == 0`, all threads skip the loads but still reach the barrier; thread zero executes an empty summation loop and writes zero. The host neither initializes the device output nor skips this launch.
4. Summing at most 96 ordinary CUDA 32-bit integers fits in `long long`. The completion event is recorded after the kernel in the same stream, and synchronizing it checks completion and can report asynchronous errors. This execution check complements, rather than replaces, the synchronization and bounds proof.

B’s wait references the first recording of E, because that was the most recent recording when the wait was submitted. Recording E again does not retarget that already-submitted wait. P2 can therefore race with C1: there is no dependency ordering those two accesses to the shared allocation. I’m interpreting “weight” as “wait,” and “save first use” as “safe first use.”

A permitted execution is:
1. A executes P1, writing value 1, then completes the first recording of E.
2. B’s wait on that recording is satisfied, but C1 has not yet executed.
3. A starts P2, overwriting the allocation; B starts C1, reading the same allocation, without any ordering between them.
4. The second recording of E has not completed, so querying E returns `cudaErrorNotReady`.

That query describes the latest recording, not the recording captured by B’s earlier wait. It neither protects the allocation nor establishes whether C1 has completed. Depending on scheduling, C1 may observe overwritten or inconsistent data; this is not a valid ownership protocol.

Use two slots, each with a ready event and a consumed event. For each reuse, A waits for that slot’s previous consumed recording before overwriting it; B waits for its current ready recording before reading it. On first use, skip the consumed wait because there is no previous consumer. The following complete example uses no device-wide synchronization and no host synchronization between iterations.

```cpp
// ownership.cu
#include <cuda_runtime.h>
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>

#define CHECK(call) do {                                         \
    cudaError_t status = (call);                                 \
    if (status != cudaSuccess) {                                 \
        std::fprintf(stderr, "%s: %s\n", #call,                  \
                     cudaGetErrorString(status));                \
        std::exit(EXIT_FAILURE);                                \
    }                                                           \
} while (0)

__global__ void produce(int* slot, int value) {
    *slot = value;
}

__global__ void consume(const int* slot, int* result) {
    *result = *slot;
}

struct Slot {
    int* data = nullptr;
    cudaEvent_t ready;
    cudaEvent_t consumed;
    bool used = false;
};

int main() {
    constexpr int iterations = 8;
    cudaStream_t A, B;
    CHECK(cudaStreamCreateWithFlags(&A, cudaStreamNonBlocking));
    CHECK(cudaStreamCreateWithFlags(&B, cudaStreamNonBlocking));

    Slot slots[2];
    for (Slot& slot : slots) {
        CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&slot.data),
                         sizeof(int)));
        CHECK(cudaEventCreateWithFlags(&slot.ready,
                                        cudaEventDisableTiming));
        CHECK(cudaEventCreateWithFlags(&slot.consumed,
                                        cudaEventDisableTiming));
    }

    int* results = nullptr;
    CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&results),
                     iterations * sizeof(int)));

    for (int i = 0; i < iterations; ++i) {
        Slot& slot = slots[i % 2];

        // Capture the previous consumed recording before reuse.
        // First use has no previous consumer to wait for.
        if (slot.used) {
            CHECK(cudaStreamWaitEvent(A, slot.consumed, 0));
        }

        produce<<<1, 1, 0, A>>>(slot.data, i + 1);
        CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
        CHECK(cudaEventRecord(slot.ready, A));

        // Capture this iteration's ready recording now.
        CHECK(cudaStreamWaitEvent(B, slot.ready, 0));
        consume<<<1, 1, 0, B>>>(slot.data, results + i);
        CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
        CHECK(cudaEventRecord(slot.consumed, B));
        slot.used = true;
    }

    // Final completion only: no host waits occurred between iterations.
    // B's final completion also implies all producers have completed,
    // because every consumer follows its corresponding ready event.
    CHECK(cudaStreamSynchronize(B));

    int host_results[iterations];
    CHECK(cudaMemcpy(host_results, results, sizeof(host_results),
                     cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost));
    for (int i = 0; i < iterations; ++i) {
        if (host_results[i] != i + 1) {
            std::fprintf(stderr, "iteration %d: got %d, expected %d\n",
                         i, host_results[i], i + 1);
            return EXIT_FAILURE;
        }
    }

    for (Slot& slot : slots) {
        CHECK(cudaEventDestroy(slot.ready));
        CHECK(cudaEventDestroy(slot.consumed));
        CHECK(cudaFree(slot.data));
    }
    CHECK(cudaFree(results));
    CHECK(cudaStreamDestroy(A));
    CHECK(cudaStreamDestroy(B));
    std::puts("All results passed.");
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

Compile and run with `nvcc -std=c++17 ownership.cu -o ownership && ./ownership` on a CUDA-capable system.

The ownership proof is the per-slot chain `producer → ready → consumer → consumed → next producer`. Stream order places each ready recording after its producer and each consumed recording after its consumer; explicit waits supply the cross-stream edges. Each wait is submitted before its target event is recorded again, so it captures the intended generation even if the host queues later iterations before earlier GPU work finishes. Thus each slot remains unchanged throughout its consumer’s read, and first use is safe because the first producer initializes the allocation before any consumer accesses it.

